     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Patel Cunningham and Miller Clinic\n\nPatient: Melanie Patrick DOB: 11/16/1976 Sex: F MRN: 458828\n\nEncounters:\n2026-01-04 Peripheral arterial disease / aortic aneurysm BP 118/67 Wt 169 lbs\n2025-06-02 rash BP 112/66 Wt 175 lbs\n2024-10-29 Peripheral arterial disease / aortic aneurysm BP 118/71 Wt 174 lbs\n2024-03-27 Peripheral arterial disease / aortic aneurysm BP 118/64 Wt 167 lbs\n2023-08-23 back strain BP 114/72 Wt 167 Peripheral arterial disease / aortic aneurysm BP 109/64 Wt 169 lbs\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n\n• Peripheral arterial disease / aortic aneurysm (abdominal aortic aneurysm, smoker, 5.9 cm) - controlled; no medications currently prescribed.\n\n The patient continues to present with controlled peripheral arterial disease and an abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 5.9 cm. The aneurysm size has remained stable since its last measurement. The patient is a smoker and has been counseled on smoking multiple times. She has been compliant with follow-up appointments and imaging studies to monitor the aneurysm. Currently, medical management is being utilized to control her condition without the need for surgical intervention. She continues to maintain a regular exercise regimen, running 3-4 times a week.\n\n• Rash - resolved, no further intervention needed.\n\n The patient presented with a rash in June 2025, which was evaluated and treated appropriately. The rash has since resolved, and no further action is required.\n\n• Vital signs: Blood pressure has ranged from 109/64 to 118/71, and weight has ranged from 167 to 175 pounds.\n\n The patient's blood pressure and weight have been relatively stable over the past few years, with slight fluctuations within normal ranges. \n\n• Social history: Alcohol consumption approximately 3 drinks per week.\n\n The patient reports consuming around 3 alcoholic drinks per week. She has been counseled on the potential risks associated with alcohol consumption, especially given her cardiovascular condition.\n\nOverall, Melanie Patrick has been managing her cardiovascular conditions effectively with appropriate lifestyle modifications and regular monitoring. She remains under close surveillance to ensure the stability of her aortic aneurysm and to manage her peripheral arterial disease conservatively.\n"
